Z||000031||Mach10||10-06-2001||11:36 PM||||Man, I disappear for 3 days and look what happens?!?<P>Ray: Let me CLARIFY your statement  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Religion isn't evil, it's the practice of it. Throughout history, Religion has been the cause of so much anguish, torture, death, and destruction. I kinda agree with the statement, and will push it a little further... Without a sense of "good," there can't be "evil." Makes you wonder, huh?<BR>The MAJOR problem is, for most every time that religion gets involved in major conflict, or other human suffering, it's always a relatively bunch of crazed lunatics that are behind it. 9-11, Salem, Spanish Inquisition, and so on. <P>So yes, indirectly, Religion is the root of all evil. Personally, I prefer Faith to religion. Organized religion is a very dangerous thing. I've dabbled in various forms of christianity. Catholicism, Mennonitism, and few others. For easy of use, I'm an "Agnostic Catholic" (I can't bring myself to hate gay people or people of a different faith than my own... Makes me a bad catholic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) Faith comes from within, and is pure. Religion is institutionalizing it, and organizing people together. That is a VERY dangerous thing...<P>When you band people together, and acustomize them into parrallel thinking along a set of loose rules (another touche, Ray...) that are completely open to interpretation, it's VERY easy to make 'em stray. The monsters in our lives are products of society. That's not to say TV, media, Games, but as of Society as a whole... Let's jump into some basics of cultural anthropology: Did you know that there has never been a single "civilization" that didn't have a belief system completely incorporated into their mode of living? Face it. Humans can survive very nicely "in the wild." Hunting-gathering is SO much easier than agriculture. Without religion, there isn't really a point to joinin up bands, and creating cities. H-G living cannot support a dense population. The population will stay in stasis according to the food supply. Now, toss in a religion, with a set of rules. Suddenly, you have a large group of people thinking the same way. <P>Let's say there is a sacred rock? So they move camps near it. All the game is gone, but someone figures out that if you work your ass off on this piece of flat land, you can make enough bread for everyone, and have plenty of leftover for the winter, while the other guy figures out how to tame goats. Here come the working class. You now have a full agricultural revolution. Immediately, the society stratifies. The existance of "surplus" allows people to breed faster (ask if you want an explanation. It's neat, but long), and the people controling the surplus quickly rise to the top. Behold, the nobility. However, rising faster are the guys who FOUND the sacred rock, and the ones that "hear" the rock's orders. Everyone respects them. Welcome to the ruling priest caste. They tell EVERYONE what to do. What to plant, who to mate with, where to build. Cool, huh? Now, suddenly, they start looking abroad. We need more space. Oh, look! Another holy rock, with it's OWN town. Looks like a nice spot. Let's tell everyone that the rock says that those guys are devils, and must be destroyed, and the rock pushed into a lake to quell it's bad influence. Nifty. A religious war. Welcome to civiliazation!<P>It's only been fairly recently that society has been able to function without a priest caste. Take the Aztecs. The only reason they got so big was that their religious practices (cannibalism. Again, ask if you want more details) allowed them the food they needed to become huge, and the fear it instilled kept the populace in line. Without the bloodthirsty priesthood, there would be no reason for people to stick together and form these HUGE cities. (Teotihuacan: Pop. 300,000)<P>After several thousand years of religion governing life, population grew to the point that H-G just wasn't an option in the urbanized countries. After a while, the emphasis on ruling became more based on wealth, or military power. As people expanded, information trade improved. With more information of other people, and other cultures coming in, the church had less and less political power over the people; it could no longer easily tell people who to kill, without some hefty resistance. The only real reason we still live in cities is that our civilization developed such momentum that it is almost impossible to stop. People aren't born farmers anymore. Everyone can be whatever they want. By being anything but a hunter-gatherer, you are only adding to the momentum...<P>So, back onto religion, you can see what a major part of us it has always been. Just because it doesn't have as big a hand in politics as it used to (our code of laws still are based on Christian world-views and taboos), it still has the ability to exert the same power over people. Now, the pope can't order another crusade. Realistically, most of the US would just change channels. But the devout Catholics, who DO believe that the Pope is the king of the church will grab whatever they can and run screaming at the foes. It's these kind of people that are dangerous. In a way, they are almost beatific. At one with themselves, at one with their faith, and totally loyal to their church. A perfect existance, with ample reward at the end. The trouble starts when someone in a position of power over them (i.e., preist) twists a passage to mean something violent to their neighbors, and gives them a good reason to attack, and is quick enough with words to negate any other passages decrying the act.<P><BR>MJ: In answer to your question, yes. We all are. We live our life one way, someone doesn't like that. You can go through all the connections, and determine what you want. At the moment, I can only say to blame whoever put the plan in motion. At the moment, the popular vote is Osama Bin Laden. Assuming that the US DOES have hard evidence for his guilt, he should be charged for this attack. <P>Anyone else want to see the Taliban turned into Chunky salsa? I liked the footage of that Taliban soldier taking out that woman's legs with a lead pipe. Islam isn't the problem; The woman was muslim too. It's fundamentalism. Fundamentalism in any form (Taliban, Green Peace, IRA, etc., ) is extremely dangerous, and should be quelled immediately. As much as people can whine about the US being a police force, and such. I ask them: Who else will do it? You don't like it? Tough. Behave like civilized people, and we'll talk. The US might not be popular by taking down the taliban (and any other group like it) but things like this need to be done. If the taliban was quelled 5 years ago, there wouldn't be this problem. Afghan would not be the terror-haven it is now. ||||reg||6
Z||000032||DaRkLoRD||10-07-2001||12:01 AM||||hmm.. maybe there's some stuff I'm not aware of, but comparing green peace to the taliban seems a bit extreme..<P>anyway, I've also read about and seen photos of the "ancient spark plug". It's rather crude, only a spark plug by principle, not like what we have today, but it would work. the only thing it implies, is that WE ARE NOT THE FIRST ADVANCED CIVILIZATION TO INHABIT THIS PLANET! nothing mysterious, and certainly nothing to do with ANY religion... it's not mainstream science, but there's no reason it couldn't be..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
