Z||000055||Patrick's Dad||10-07-2001||09:21 PM||||MJ: Yeah, I take a lot of strange turns in conversation, and sometimes in my driving, too. It makes life more interesting. My input was a defense against painting all people of faith as lunatics, ready to kill all that aren't like them. The point was well made by others that there's plenty of killing going on by those who have no god.<P>JohnnyK: Am I a new earth type? I don't know. I know that the Hebrew word for "day" (yom) is kind of specific in its meaning. There is a roundabout sort of "proof" in the Bible (I haven't dug nearly deep enough to say that I endorse this or not) that the Flood was not the first worldwide catastrophe, that there was a sort of "second creation." God created man and woman in Gen I:26, then it is redescribed in Gen II:7-24. Some see this as a reconciliation of new/old earth. Then again, "Isn't 1 second worth 1 million years in God's kingdom? (Mach10)" Still working on it.<P>My problem with not taking the Bible literally (unless clearly a parable) is, where do we draw that line? No one has legitimately refuted the life, miracles, death and resurrection of Christ (No, the Jesus seminar is not legitimate....). The dead sea scrolls talk of His followers. OTOH, to paraphrase Paul (probably badly), if we are wrong, then what have we wroght? If we follow the principals that Jesus laid down, and there is no afterlife, then we should at least have left the world a little better than we found it. If there is an afterlife, and we scoff at it, then we are truly miserable, in life, and in death.<P>I think I'll go have a good cup of tea.||||reg||1
