Z||000063||EdsB52||10-09-2001||04:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Well, I'm sorry that you felt the need to delete the link. There was nothing of a religious nature anywhere in the linked page, as far as I can remember. I'm glad I still had it up in another browser window, from earlier today. <P>I don't understand what set Ray off. IMHO, his comment had nothing to do with the thread.<BR>I will say this, though...<BR>The God that I believe in is a loving God. He has never told me or anyone else that I know to go out and kill anybody in His name. <BR>I believe that we are given a free choice in how we lead our lives. It's <I>nobody's</I> job to browbeat anybody else into following their beliefs.<P><BR>Anyway, I thought the link was a good one. I will pass it on, elsewhere. <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"It's <I>nobody's</I> job to browbeat anybody else into following their beliefs."<P>No, but I think some people use religion and Christianity as a device to persuade and sometimes coerce the secular world into following either their beliefs, or be confined to the constraints of their beliefs through legislation. I think ray went too far with his assessment of religion, especially in that he blanketed all religion everywhere, but the essence of what he is alluding to is correct. For example, ray alludes to the assertion that coercion is one-way from the religious sector to the secular populous. He's right. What examples can anyone produce that the secular population deplores the rights and rituals of religious people, and proactively tries to modify the public or private behavior of said religious people? I can produce example after another where the US religious sector continually attempts (with some success) to modify the rights of secular people. To amend ray's narrow scope of people's beliefs, I would like to introduce the agnostic population of American society that doesn't hate religion and feels they should have the right to worship, as long as they don't modify the rights of others not affiliated with their beliefs. The issues of morality that Christian America tries to modify are homosexuality, promiscuity, pornography, religious decoration in government areas, infiltration of religion into public arenas for the purpose of proliferation, and rights of personal choice. What rights do secular people try to revoke from religious people? I don't think all religions are evil, but I do believe the application of the Muslim religion is often evil. And when the American religious-right lobbies for the right to public prayer for religion, do they also mean Satanists, or do they mean exclusively Christianity when they refer to religion? I think the latter. I don't believe Christian America's agendas are objective or pure, I believe they are very coercive. America and the Constitution is about freedom of choice; contemporary American Christianity is about confinement to Christian beliefs; hard to believe the Constitution was written when Christianity had an extreme strong-hold on America. I would assert American Christianity has changed its value in America from its original conception.<P>"In any religion, it's the intolerant fanatics that cause all the problems and, unfortunately, get all the press. The fanatics will use religion as an excuse to support their <I>political</I> agenda. They seem to think it will justify their actions."<P>You kind of switched gears here. First you were talking about religious fanatics in the context of murderous and radical behavior, then you made reference to a political agenda, which are you addressing? I agree with you that the kooks will still perform their heinous deeds in the name of some cause or religion, but the religious right is the only group I can think of that uses religion in a political fashion. Maybe you were using the word "political" as a metaphor??? Don't forget, we fashion our God in our image, as everyone else does, not the other way around.||||reg||1
Z||000064||EdsB52||10-09-2001||04:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mbramble:<BR><B> What <I>was</I> that little guy's name over in Germany 50 or so years ago??  Was he killing all those Jews because <I>he</I> was the believer and <I>they</I> were not??  <P>Or maybe I don't understand what you are talking about since you can't put together a comprehensible sentence.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That was in response to this:<P>"atheists do not kill belivers......"<P>I agree with you that in past times it was fashionable for the secular population to justify the killing of Christians for the sake of killing Christians (best example would be the Romans), but in contemporary times it seems that people are killing the secular crowd with the rationalization of Christian beliefs. That doesn't mean Christianity is evil or that it is to blame, just that the tables of rationalization have turned.<P>"What <I>was</I> that little guy's name over in Germany 50 or so years ago??  Was he killing all those Jews because <I>he</I> was the believer and <I>they</I> were not??"<P>Did Hitler target the Jews because of religion, or due to their physical attributes? I don't know gobs about pre-WWII Europe, but I don't believe the Holocaust was religiously based. Didn't Germany invade Poland in 1939 for the purpose of occupation and found the Jews to be cumbersome and in the way, so he wanted to annihilate them? Someone fill in the blanks, but I don't Hitler’s actions were religiously based, as were the Romans. In defense of your position is an example of religious persecution, and that's WACO. Reno attacked because she feared some kind of uprising of a small group, but there were religious overtones there.||||reg||1
Z||000065||EdsB52||10-09-2001||04:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Dennis: There is a god and you should do a reality check? Thats a pretty bold statement with no background evidence isn't it.  Such thigns like gravity and air are proved through science. IE, doing experiments over and over while controlling the variables so that the result is a 99.9% rate of conclusiveness (sp).  You show me an experiment that I can repeat over and over proving of a higher power and I will be all for it. However, try telling a religious man that science can disprove religion, and he will get all angry. See, science looks for answers, even if they are spiritual and religious. Religion would not dare let science prove that there is no higher power (did that come out as I wanted it to?)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is a beautiful point and the crux of my criticisms about religion, especially Christianity. In science, everything is up for grabs. Our atmosphere is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and 1% other. If you have a theory that defies this basic premise of our planet, science and scientists will line up to eagerly hear it and debate it. With Christianity, if you question the Bible, God's existence, Jesus’ existence, or the history of the writings within the Bible, you will chastised, condemned straight to fncking hell, and possibly excommunicated by the religious (Christian) sector. A little animation, but I think you get the idea. NOW CAREFULLY READ THIS......Christianity is coercive. Science begs you to differ; Christianity dares you to differ. Which one bears more credibility based on those premises? Honestly, debate the issues presented in the argument, not the character of those arguing. I'm agnostic because I believe it takes an ignorant man to label something as fact when we have little evidence to prove or disprove them. What I will assert that is provable is the fact that American Christianity has an agenda to modify the behavior of other Americans that don't affect the lives of Christian America. Who cares what kind of music people listen to? Who cares if Billy-Bob is jamming Scotty in the turd-shoot (unless you're one of them)? Who cares if kids wear a uniform to school? What American Christianity does is to remove the individuality from the youth of America to which I believe they inherently have a birthright. Are we going to protect them from themselves to the point of diminishing most liberty? This is not the direction I'd like to see America going, and the Christian right seems to be convoluting Christianity, morality, and statute to the point of corruption, collusion, and intrusion.||||reg||1
Z||000066||EdsB52||10-09-2001||05:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I'm not bashing anyone. I'm just looking over all the facts. And there still doesn't seem to be one solid piece of evidence supporting creation/god, etc.. I'm honestly just looking objectively at it all.  Dennis: I want to know this way to "look for" and find god. And something concrete perhaps. Not just your usual "open your heart" stuff.<P>Are you saying you don't believe this world is millions of years old? Personally I've never heard of this "spark plug" thing, and I think it's probably just a legend, or some random mishap where they found a sparkplug.  But I've never heard of it. Nobody ACTUALLY believes this earth is only a few thousand years old?!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Dennis: I want to know this way to "look for" and find god. And something concrete perhaps. Not just your usual "open your heart" stuff."<P>Exactly, me too. All I ever hear or read is follow your heart, yet Christians sell their beliefs as fact. I would live to hear all challenges of science or Christianity, but in the form of historical or physical proof. The few Christians I talk to that label their beliefs as merely faith have my respect, the majority of Christians that are ready to throw down if anyone dare question the presence of God and Jesus have my pity. That's right, I pity the fools! The same goes with big bangers, evolutionists, or reincarnationists. I cautiously subscribe to Darwinian evolution, but the chain is broken in several places. Darwinian evolution proves small sections of fact based on observations of animals and their adaptation to their environment. Christianity based on the writings of the Bible illustrate the writings of many people from more than 2,000 years ago, as written in Hebrew and Latin translated into English in 1455 (so I'm told). It's a very old story with nothing to reinforce it but itself and the historical references it cites. <P>As far as the age of the earth; Christianity says 6,000 years while science says 3.8-4.7 billion. If the earth has been here only 6,000 years, doesn't that refute the existence of dinosaurs 60 million years ago? Of course Christians attack the dating methods of scientists (carbon, isotopes), but if they have nothing more to offer up than something about a spark plug in rock sediment then I would say they must acquiesce the point of dating techniques or scientifically disprove the methods that are currently accepted by the scientific community by way of establishing other methods. But within Christianity you dare not question the word or authority of your elders. The beauty of science is that a 15-year-old kid could refute something Einstein created or discovered and be adored by all scientists of all ages. In religion and Christianity, if you circumvent the pecking order it's your ass.||||reg||1
Z||000067||EdsB52||10-09-2001||05:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>where can I find out more about this mysterious spark plug? Was it a Champion by any chance?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think the guy in New Mexico with the image of Jesus in his tortilla found it. He said it was still good and installed it in his Yugo where it still resides. He claims to get great gas mileage and feels like every time he passes smog, there must be some kind of divine intervention.||||reg||1
Z||000068||EdsB52||10-09-2001||05:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>ancient sparkplug..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.eskimo.com/~pierres/coso/coso.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eskimo.com/~pierres/coso/coso.html</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Very little is known about the initial physical inspections of the artifact. According to discoverer Virginia Maxey, a geologist she spoke with who examined the fossil shells encrusting the specimen said the nodule had taken at least 500,000 years to attain its present form. However, the identity of the first geologist is still a mystery, and his findings were never officially published."<P>I didn't read the entire article, but doesn't this passage disprove the 'short-earth' (6,000 years) theory?||||reg||1
