Z||000078||Mach10||10-09-2001||10:59 PM||||3.5 Million Christians killed by Muslims? Did I miss something?!?? Show me where...<P>It's interesting... Most religions prefer to kill within their own group: The Taliban excecutes more muslims than christians... Christians have been murdering black and gay christians for years... Funny, I can't think of any good intra-religiou Judaic attrocities... Anyone got any? <P>Let's face it. Most religions PREACH peace and forgiveness etc. What is practiced is entirely different. Let's skip this "Holier than Thou" facade and admit that we are every bit as blood-thirsty as everyone else.<BR>I'll be the first to admit that the Catholic church is responsible for almost as many deaths in the middle ages as the Black Plague. Our church hates gay people, and any sort of family planning. I know, I'm a large critic! I can't STAND it. So that's why I have faith. **** the church. I'll go to mass, and I'll take blood/body. I'll pray. I bend my knee. But I won't let the church determine my ideals. There, that's what I percieve as evil in my church. What's YOUR church done?  <P>Terry Pratchett Quote: "The time comes periodically for the altar to be cleansed. Blood is usually considered appropriate for this" (Small Gods)||||reg||1
Z||000079||87GTBro||10-09-2001||11:24 PM||||This is an interesting conversation and I am not going to get involved in it other than to say this one thing...<P>All of you are right in some of your points.  Religion, in and of itself, is evil.  I am NOT religious.  Religion, by definition, is a set of rules set up by man governing beliefs and actions pertaining to being "holy".  <P>Many people confuse the term "religion" with anybody that believes there is a God or has a Faith of any kind.  If you ask any professing "nondenominational" Christian if they are religious, they will tell you "NO".  I am one of those.  I want nothing to do with religion.  I follow God's laws as written in the Bible (the original one, and only one as far as I am concerned).  Religion is a bunch of other laws conceived by man, who is evil by nature (born into sin).  Religion is where these man-made laws Osama bin Laden came up with came from.  "Kill all Americans because they stand in the way of us (Muslims) taking over the world?"  Does that sound like something God would do?<P>I mean think of it.  You have to admit that there is a God (unless you agree your great grandfather was an ape, that's fine with me. I wouldn't say it too loud though).  So if God created man, why would He be telling His creation to kill other people He created?<P>That makes just as much sense as a mother having twin boys and raising them until they are mature and convincing one of the twins that the other is evil and that he must be killed.  I know that is not a good analogy since we have a lot of psycho people running the streets nowadays, but assume the mother loved both of the boys equally.  How much sense does it make?<P>I remember just after the attacks a Muslim man living in New York was interviewed on the streets.  The reporter asked him, "Do you believe this act was pertpetrated by Muslims?"  He responded by saying, "No, this is not Muslim.  Muslim is no kill people!"<P>Osama is not Muslim.  Just like David Koresh was not Christian.  Just like the Jones guy wasn't Christian.  The definition of Christian is someone who follows and wants to be like Jesus Christ.  I don't recall Jesus ever leading whole crowds of people to drink poison and die, or grab a whole bunch of weapons to kill the people that came against him.  One of his disciples, Peter, did raise his sword and cut off the ear of a soldier while trying to protect Jesus just before he was crucified.  But what did Jesus do?  He picked up the soldier's ear, and put it back in place and it was healed.<P>If you read the Bible in the last book of Revelation, you will read about false prophets that will come saying they are believers with the intent to lead many astray.  Osama bin Laden is a false prophet of the Muslim world.  His beliefs are completely tangent from the original Muslim beliefs.  He has essentially started his own religion and his followers are worshipping HIM and not Allah.  He may refference Allah and give Him credit, but that's what a false prophet does.  He's a fraud.  <P>Bin Laden is NOT Muslim and therefore this whole argument cannot be blamed or aimed at religion!  He is just an off-the-wall psychotic killer trying to "take over the world" with the Muslim religion as his facade.<P>It is so easy to hide behind religion.  That's what causes so much division in the world.<P>I said more than I wanted to say, but I hope you understand the point I was trying to get across.  You can take it, or you can leave it, but please don't be ignorant.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig_3.gif">  <BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto||||reg||1
Z||000080||Mach10||10-09-2001||11:50 PM||||*applaus*<P>Well put!<P>Except for the evolution thing. But we can sling back and forth all week and all we'll end up doing is wearing out my textbooks, and none of us will believe any different. All I can say is that I've held physical evidence in my hands. A translated and re-translated text will NEVER be able to compare to that... Now, whether my evidence is valid? I wasn't here that long ago. I can only go by what I know, and what I suspect, and what I can substantiate. <BR>I don't believe that the bible was meant to be taken literally. But it teaches some good things, and by those things I live my life. Regardless whether I'm descended from a monkey, or an idiot named "Adam"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000081||Tonker||10-10-2001||01:00 AM||||Interesting discussion. I'll admit right up front that I'm not particularly religious. It's not that I don't believe in God, I really don't know one way or another. Realistically I'm not going to know until such time as I hear:<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>G- *BING* <FONT SIZE="3">Tonker...</FONT> I want you to build me an Ark. <BR>T- What?! Who's there???<BR>G- It's the Lord, Tonker.<BR>T- <I>Right</I>... Who is this <I>really</I>?<BR>...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't think you can catigorically state that religion is bad. The essential messages of religions are normally very good. I mean, really, "Thou shalt not kill" is hardly a bad ideal, is it? Religion also brings hope and comfort to many people. Still not a bad thing.<P>Where there is a problem is when there is blind obedience or dedication to a faith. This is particularly bad when people refuse to temper their faith with common sense and indepedent thought. Faith should guide you, not compel you, in my opinion. <P>Many terrible things have been done in the name of religion, but at the same time many horrible things have been done in the name of science. Should we stop trying to expand our knowledge of the way the universe works because of it? When people follow an ideal blindly is where the problems emerge. Horrible deeds may be done in the <I>name of religion</I> without it being in the <I>spirit of religion</I>.<P>I don't know how Christianity or Islam or Buddism emerged. It could be the word of God, it could be the word of some drunk hiding behind a bush he had just set on fire. Personally I've always suspected that religion had a lot to do with trying to develop laws and a system of governance for more primitive societies by playing to people's fear of death and the unknown, but it's quite likely that I'm wrong. One thing to remember with most of these religions is that they were initially based in oral tradition and not written down at the time the events "ocurred". As a result, details may have changed with each telling so that the real truth of the events becomes hidden behind historical inaccuracies.<P>To be honest you'd have a hard time convincing me that the world was created in seven days as described in the bible. You'd have to bring some strong proof to refute what science has already shown me. Does that make the Bible wrong? Maybe. However, you can instead look at what is written in the Bible as a metaphor. It's quite possible that it is correct in that respect. As I understand it they order things are created in the Bible are interestingly close to they way they might have evolved. Also it's possible - and likely that the bible is basically the story about the history and journeys of one group of people and not about all of humanity. The similarities between the stories of Christianity, Judaism and Islam are close enough that there must be some truth to them, I think.<P>To summarize, I don't think religion itself is bad. Blind pursuit of religion is unhealthy, as is blind pursuit of any other ideal. Now lets all go have some milk and cookies.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 10-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000082||maryjane||10-10-2001||01:05 AM||||Re "Now let's all go have some milk & cookies"<BR>Best thing I've read in this thread yet.||||reg||1
